    id: "flow-control", title: "Flow Control", difficulty: "medium",
    definition: "Flow control prevents a fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver. Stop-and-Wait: sender sends one frame, waits for ACK, then sends next — simple but slow (one frame in flight). Sliding Window: sender can have multiple unacknowledged frames in flight (the window size), dramatically improving throughput. The receiver advertises its buffer capacity (window). Flow control is receiver-driven; congestion control is network-driven.",
    coreUnderstanding: "Flow control prevents a fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver. If the sender sends faster than the receiver can process, frames are dropped. Stop-and-Wait is the simplest: send one frame, wait for an acknowledgment (ACK), then send the next. It's simple but slow — only one frame is in flight at a time. Sliding Window is the efficient solution: the sender can have multiple unacknowledged frames in flight (the window size), dramatically improving throughput. The receiver advertises its buffer capacity (window size), and the sender adjusts. Flow control is receiver-driven (the receiver's buffer is the concern); congestion control is network-driven (the network's capacity is the concern).",
    howItWorks: "1. Stop-and-Wait: send one frame, wait for ACK, send next. Simple but slow (one frame in flight, idle while waiting).\n2. Sliding Window: sender can have N unacknowledged frames in flight (window size N). Dramatically faster.\n3. The receiver advertises its buffer capacity (receive window). The sender can't send more than the window allows.\n4. As ACKs arrive, the window slides forward, allowing new frames to be sent.\n5. If the receiver's buffer fills, it advertises a smaller window (or 0). The sender stops or slows down.\n6. Flow control is receiver-driven (the receiver's buffer is the concern). Congestion control is network-driven (the network's capacity).\n7. TCP uses sliding window flow control: the receive window (rwnd) in the TCP header tells the sender how much buffer the receiver has.",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "text", code: "-- Stop-and-Wait (simple, slow):\n-- Sender                    Receiver\n--   |-- frame 1 -->|\n--   |               | (process)\n--   |<-- ACK 1 ----|\n--   |-- frame 2 -->|\n--   |               | (process)\n--   |<-- ACK 2 ----|\n-- Only one frame in flight at a time. Slow (idle while waiting for ACK).\n\n-- Sliding Window (efficient, window size = 4):\n-- Sender                    Receiver\n--   |-- frame 1 -->|\n--   |-- frame 2 -->|        (can send up to 4 without ACK)\n--   |-- frame 3 -->|\n--   |-- frame 4 -->|\n--   |<-- ACK 1 ----|        (window slides: can send frame 5)\n--   |-- frame 5 -->|\n--   |<-- ACK 2 ----|        (window slides: can send frame 6)\n--   |-- frame 6 -->|\n-- Multiple frames in flight = much higher throughput.\n\n-- Receive window (receiver advertises buffer capacity):\n-- Receiver buffer: [####____] (4 of 8 slots used)\n-- Advertised window: 4 (can receive 4 more frames)\n-- If buffer fills: [########] → advertised window: 0 (stop sending)\n-- As receiver processes: [##______] → advertised window: 6 (can send more)\n\n-- TCP flow control:\n-- The TCP header includes the receive window (rwnd).\n-- rwnd = receiver's available buffer space.\n-- Sender can't send more than rwnd bytes unacknowledged.\n-- If rwnd = 0, the sender stops (or sends probe packets).", output: "-- Stop-and-Wait: one frame in flight, simple but slow.\n-- Sliding Window: multiple frames in flight, efficient.\n-- Receiver advertises buffer capacity (window).", note: "Flow control prevents a fast sender from overwhelming a slow receiver. Stop-and-Wait is simple but slow. Sliding Window allows multiple frames in flight, dramatically improving throughput." }],
    marginNotes: ["Flow control is receiver-driven (the receiver's buffer is the concern). Congestion control is network-driven (the network's capacity). They're different mechanisms.", "Sliding window allows multiple frames in flight, dramatically improving throughput over stop-and-wait. The window size is the key parameter.", "The receiver advertises its buffer capacity (receive window). If the buffer fills, the window shrinks to 0, and the sender stops."],
    commonMistakes: ["Confusing flow control (receiver-driven, prevent overwhelming the receiver) with congestion control (network-driven, prevent overwhelming the network).", "Forgetting that stop-and-wait has only one frame in flight — it's simple but very slow (idle while waiting for ACK).", "Not understanding that the receive window is advertised by the receiver — the sender adjusts based on it."],
